WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:05.160
In nearly every corner of the earth, ants wage war against each other.

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:11.880
Their weapons are what nature gave them. Some have strong armor, deathly stingers, or sharp mandibles.

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:15.540
And then there's this
tiny and not very impressive ant,

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:19.920
but it rules the biggest empire any ant has ever built.

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:24.980
A colony spanning continents and fighting wars that leave millions of casualties.

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:28.040
Let's take a look at this unlikely warrioress,

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:31.400
"Linepithema humile"  
the Argentine ant.

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:39.240
♫ Kurzgesagt intro music ♫

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:46.660
This story begins in the floodplains 
around the Paraná River, in South America,

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:52.520
It's a crowded ant megalopolis where dozens of ant species fight for dominance,

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:58.140
including fire ants, army ants and the rather unimpressive Argentine ant.

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:02.900
It measures only 2 to 3 millimeters in length and with its small mandibles,

00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:07.060
it's surprising that it survived among its buff competitors.

00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:09.560
Their homes are equally unremarkable.

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:14.060
Their colonies range from fairly small to very large 
and could be found anywhere:

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:19.300
Under logs, in loose leaf litter
or the former colonies of other ants.

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:25.360
Here, Argentine ants prepare their most
effective weapon against their competitors: bodies.

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:29.020
Most ant species have
only one queen to produce ants,

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:32.520
while Argentine ants went all-in on numbers.

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:35.920
For every 120 workers there's one queen,

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:38.400
laying up to 60 eggs a day.

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:44.500
So their colonies grow fast and have millions or billions of individuals.

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:50.080
Teams of queens and workers frequently branch out and found new colonies.

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:55.180
But this strategy has a downside: 
As colonies grow and produce a lot of offspring,

00:01:55.180 --> 00:01:59.000
mutations occur and new colonies adapt to new environments.

00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:04.280
Their DNA slowly changes from generation to generation and differences accumulate.

00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:07.120
So after a while the ants that left the colony

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:12.260
will become more like distant cousins and start to compete with their mother colony.

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:17.180
In their native South American range, this is how Argentine ants behave.

00:02:17.180 --> 00:02:21.460
Within their colonies they are very cooperative
and well-organized,

00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:27.380
but they fight vicious wars against other Argentine ant colonies and other ant species

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:30.020
With equally strong opponents on every side,

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:35.320
the Argentine ant became extremely
aggressive, fighting for every inch of ground.

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:40.460
But it could never dominate its neighbours...
 until humans showed up.

00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:44.720
We did what humans do and transported things around the world by ship.

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:48.840
On one of them, a few Argentine ant queens hitched a ride as stowaways

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:51.740
from South America to Madeira and New Orleans.

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:57.000
The Argentine ants suddenly found themselves in a strange world.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:59.780
Instead of being surrounded by deadly enemies,

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:03.960
they found only victims ⁠— 
nobody could fight them effectively.

00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:09.100
Because only a few Argentine ant queens
were introduced to the outside world,

00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:13.120
the resulting colonies had very low genetic diversity.

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:15.940
On top of that, the introduced Argentine ants

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:19.000
kill up to 90% of their queens every year.

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:22.060
Fewer queens, less genetic variation.

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:25.000
So, as these colonies spread across the landscape,

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:29.280
ants that left the colony were no longer
considered distant cousins.

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:37.240
As a result, the new colonies form not opposing but cooperating parties called  "supercolonies".

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:40.880
This is a very uncommon
strategy in the ant kingdom,

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:45.840
only a few of the 16,000 ant species have evolved supercolonies.

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:49.560
A supercolony was established on the
West coast of the USA

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:53.520
and became a base for the tiny ants' global conquest.

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:59.760
Today, the Argentine ant inhabits the Mediterranean zones of six continents and many islands.

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:02.480
This one supercolony was especially successful,

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:08.560
establishing sister locations in California, Europe, Japan, New Zealand and Australia,

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:13.500
forming one massive intercontinental megacolony of Argentine ants.

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:19.020
This makes them  the largest society on Earth,
more numerous than even the human one.

00:04:19.020 --> 00:04:23.060
But their success has changed the ecosystems they invaded.

00:04:23.660 --> 00:04:26.720
California is a perfect example of this.

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:28.860
In their greed for more territory,

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:34.600
the invading Argentine ants have overrun and replaced 90% of the native ant species,

00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:37.960
including several species of Californian carpenter ants.

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:41.280
Although carpenter ant workers are giants,

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:45.300
their colonies have only between 3,000 and 6,000 individuals

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:50.780
and stand no chance against an expanding supercolony of billions of Argentine ants.

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:55.840
Argentine ant workers attack by wiping toxic chemicals on their victims

00:04:55.840 --> 00:05:00.980
which irritates the enemy and marks them as a target for other Argentine ants.

00:05:00.980 --> 00:05:04.380
When they attack, the Argentine ants wash over their victims,

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:08.800
clinging on to their opponents in groups 
and pulling apart their limbs.

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:12.020
It doesn't matter how many of them die ⁠— 
there are always more.

00:05:12.940 --> 00:05:15.560
Once the colony is overrun and exterminated,

00:05:15.560 --> 00:05:21.100
the Argentine ants feed on their victims brood and take over their home and territory.

00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:27.080
The Argentine ants' numbers allow them to hunt down and devour such an excessive mass of different insects

00:05:27.080 --> 00:05:31.980
that over time some species disappear
from the ants' territory completely.

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:36.160
Argentine ants don't care about working
with the local flora and fauna,

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:38.060
they consume them and move on.

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:41.580
And, if their next stop happens to be human property,

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:44.680
they will rudely make themselves at home there too.

00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:50.380
They forage in dumpsters, bowls of pet food and sneak into kitchens to claim leftovers.

00:05:50.380 --> 00:05:55.480
Not just our homes: our gardens and
fields are also impacted by Argentine ants,

00:05:55.480 --> 00:05:58.820
since they tend to hordes of aphids as their cattle.

00:05:58.820 --> 00:06:04.460
The aphids feed from plants and produce a sweet honeydew, which they trade with the ants for protection.

00:06:04.460 --> 00:06:07.980
Since the ants have no major enemy to fear in their new homes,

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:11.820
the aphids thrive  and ultimately kill the plants they live on.

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:16.300
So, on top of being a major disruption
for the ecosystems they invade,

00:06:16.300 --> 00:06:20.060
they are also a huge pest for agriculture.

00:06:20.060 --> 00:06:24.200
But the rule of the Argentine ant is being challenged.

00:06:24.200 --> 00:06:28.420
Parts of the super colonies have broken off and become their own empires.

00:06:28.420 --> 00:06:31.540
A merciless civil war has broken out.

00:06:31.540 --> 00:06:36.420
For example, the Lake Hodges Supercolony has been fighting against the Very Large Colony

00:06:36.420 --> 00:06:38.900
for years in San Diego County.

00:06:38.900 --> 00:06:44.160
A massive war is going on over a dynamic front line stretching over kilometers,

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:47.500
an estimated 30 million ants die here each
year.

00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:52.820
On other fronts, an old acquaintance from the Parana River has risen from the shadows

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:55.020
Red imported fire ants,

00:06:55.020 --> 00:06:59.280
which were accidentally introduced from their
old home to the coast of Alabama,

00:06:59.820 --> 00:07:03.980
Not only are the red fire ants fierce fighters
and more than able to deal with the

00:07:03.980 --> 00:07:08.040
Argentine ant, they are also able to form
super colonies themselves.

00:07:08.420 --> 00:07:13.700
Now the old wars from their distant home have been
taken to a foreign battleground.

00:07:14.200 --> 00:07:18.280
In the southeastern US the super colonies
clashed fiercely.

00:07:18.280 --> 00:07:24.020
The Argentine ants found themselves outgunned by the fire ants. The fire ants major workers are

00:07:24.020 --> 00:07:28.800
more than twice the size of the
Argentine ants and wield venom-injecting stingers,

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:33.600
even though the Argentine ants
fought fiercely, the fire ants were too much for them.

00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:36.740
After countless lost
battles the red imported fire ant

00:07:36.740 --> 00:07:41.200
exterminated the Argentine ants super
colony from much of the southeastern US.

00:07:42.160 --> 00:07:45.780
This is one territory lost but the
Argentine ants will fight on.

00:07:46.540 --> 00:07:51.460
This amazing network of cooperating super
colonies is the biggest success in their history.

00:07:52.440 --> 00:07:55.420
And they'll not give it up because of a small defeat.

00:07:55.420 --> 00:07:59.200
They will stand their
ground against any enemy that might arise.

00:07:59.200 --> 00:08:01.520
No matter if it's on the Paraná River

00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:05.020
or on one of the large battlefields across the world.

